WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.417
Duplicates can distort an analysis, but we must define what duplicate means.

00:00:05.417 --> 00:00:09.942
In this example, order ID is treated as the business key.

00:00:09.942 --> 00:00:13.162
That means we expect one row per order.

00:00:13.162 --> 00:00:19.037
We use duplicated with subset equal to order ID and keep equals false to display every record involved

00:00:19.037 --> 00:00:24.882
in a duplicate. Then drop_duplicates keeps the first record for each repeated order ID.

00:00:24.882 --> 00:00:28.457
Click Run and inspect the output.

00:00:28.457 --> 00:00:31.262
Now consider an important real-world question.

00:00:31.262 --> 00:00:35.952
What if the table represents order line items instead of orders?

00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:42.007
One order ID could legitimately appear several times because the customer purchased several products.

00:00:42.007 --> 00:00:47.932
In that case, removing repeated order IDs would destroy valid data.

00:00:47.932 --> 00:00:49.637
This is why row grain matters.

00:00:49.637 --> 00:00:51.847
Grain means what one row represents.

00:00:51.847 --> 00:00:56.712
Before removing duplicates, state the grain explicitly.

00:00:56.712 --> 00:01:00.162
One row per order? One row per customer?

00:01:00.162 --> 00:01:01.867
One row per product within an order?

00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:08.358
Duplicate removal is only correct when it respects the meaning of the row.
